# Tenant Feature Architecture

Everything belonging to a business domain lives together under `features/<feature>/`.
Routes stay in the App Router, but they hold no logic.

Do not create Pages Router routes or introduce a second routing architecture.

## Layers

```
app/        route entries only — a stub, a layout, or a server wrapper
features/   business domains
shell/      application chrome: sidebar, header, breadcrumbs, route guard
shared/     generic UI with no business logic
lib/        infrastructure: axios, session, permissions matrix, formatting
```

Dependency direction is one-way:

- `lib/` and `shared/` must not import from `features/`, `shell/`, or `app/`.
- `features/` may import `lib/`, `shared/`, and other features.
- `shell/` may import anything; it is the composition layer.

A feature reaching into another feature is fine and expected (the item drawer
opens the brand drawer). A shared or infrastructure module reaching into a
feature is a layering bug — invert it with an adapter prop instead, the way
`ResourceAttachmentsPanel` takes `primaryImageSync`.

## Feature structure

```
features/<feature>/
  api/          HTTP calls, one named function per endpoint
  queries/      React Query hooks, query keys, cache helpers
  components/   feature-specific UI, grouped per component
  pages/        page-level components rendered by the route
  hooks/        feature-specific non-query hooks
  utils/        pure helpers
  validation/   schemas, when the feature needs them
  index.js      public API
```

Use only the folders the feature actually needs. `overview` is two files.

## Routes

Each route re-exports its page component in one line:

```js
// app/[locale]/main/brands/page.js
export { default } from "@/features/brands/pages/BrandsPage";
```

A route file may hold server-side work that must run there — reading `headers()`,
redirecting — but never business logic or data fetching.

## The barrel

`index.js` exposes API functions, query keys, and non-component utilities:

```js
export * from "./api/brands.api";
export * from "./queries/brandsQueryKeys";
```

Do not export components through the barrel. Cross-feature component imports use
the deep path (`@/features/brands/components/BrandDrawer/BrandDrawer`), which
keeps default exports intact and prevents import cycles.

Inside a feature, import siblings by relative path, never through your own barrel.

## Registering a feature

Add one entry to `FEATURES` in `features/registry.js`. The sidebar, the route
guard's module and permission checks, and the quick-create menu all derive from
it. Verify with `npm run check:registry`.

## Language and imports

The project uses JavaScript with JSDoc. Do not introduce TypeScript files unless
explicitly requested as a coordinated migration.

Use the `@/` path alias for cross-layer imports, relative paths within a feature.
